Again, you didn’t—that’s great. But it isn’t “no drug touches anything else”; it’s “the side-effect profile is low enough that many people notice nothing.” ### So where does this leave the original idea? Your experience is real, and it proves that when drugs are exquisitely selective, they can indeed feel like they fix only the problem and do nothing else. The earlier warning that “most drugs aren’t perfectly selective” was about the messy reality of drug development for complex diseases (cancer, psychiatric disorders, chronic pain), where we often can’t design a perfect lock because the target protein is similar to other vital ones. For example, ibuprofen blocks both COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes, which is why it can cause stomach issues in some people even as it relieves your headache. That’s the norm, not the exception. The fact that you’ve never felt a side effect is wonderful, but it’s a reflection of how well we’ve learned to stack the odds in favor of a clean response—not a guarantee that every drug hits only one thing.
